# Per-path token ceilings, gated by `itok check` (pinned tier, V5). Only
# registered paths are checked; everything else is unchecked (V10). This is
# itok checking itself -- the dogfood.
#
# WHAT THIS CEILING IS: a cap on ENTRY COST -- what a session loads before
# any work begins. Measured: overhead (system prompt + tool schemas)
# ~28,543 itok + SPEC.md ~26,532 = ~55,075 resident at turn one, 42% of
# gpt-oss:20b's 131,072 window and 28% of a 200k one. Entry cost is
# re-billed EVERY turn (V84/V94), so it is subtracted from headroom before
# the agent does anything. Not a fit constraint: SPEC.md is 2.5% of 1M.
#
# HEADROOM is a DIFFERENT quantity and not a synonym for the slack below:
# headroom is what is LEFT after everything is loaded (window minus used),
# which is what `itok headroom` reports (V91). Slack under this ceiling is
# maintenance pressure. Conflating them borrows a verb's precise meaning
# for a place it does not apply -- V2, inside our own vocabulary.
#
# It forces a REVIEW, not a shrink (V84). Set with real slack (~10-13%): a
# ceiling a hair above current turns every legitimate addition into a
# raise, and a threshold raised reflexively trains the reflex instead of
# catching the problem (V71). It catches RUNAWAY growth; T70's compaction
# machine pays the debt -- two jobs, and conflating them made the ceiling
# useless at both.
# The TENTH covers one session's worth of §V and §B: V114-V122, T100-T107,
# B29-B33. The badge learned what it was measuring (a level against a
# measured compaction point, growth instead of re-billing, a clock), and
# five defects fell out of building it -- each one a record, because a bug
# found by measurement and not by the suite is the part worth keeping.
#
# 52,000 against 45,973 is ~13%, at the top of the band. The number moved
# more this session than in any before it, and the honest reading is that
# the SPEC is now the dominant entry cost: ~46k re-billed every turn of
# every session, against a README of 5.1k and an overhead of ~32k. T49 and
# T70 are the standing answer and neither has run since the eighth raise.
# The next raise should be refused until one of them has.
#
# README 6,500 -> 5,800, and this is a LOWERING -- the first. The ninth
# raise was bought by four verbs' worth of surface AND by the `rate` blurb
# growing to ~2,600 characters of invariant recitation. T107 split `blurb`
# from `reference` and cut the recitation, so the block went 5,753 ->
# 5,110 and `itok --help` went 2,570 -> 623 itok. 5,800 against 5,110 is
# ~13%. A ceiling left at 6,500 over a file that shrank would stop
# catching the growth it exists for.
#
# The NINTH covers V114-V116 with their records (T100-T102, B29): the badge
# was coloring an occupancy level with a flow's absolute threshold, and the
# fix needed the auto-compact point, which turned out to be MEASURABLE --
# the harness writes `compactMetadata.preTokens` at every compaction. The
# measurements are the part that had to stay: six observed compactions
# spanning 83.5% of a 200k window to 100.1% of a 1M one are what make
# "there is no single reserve fraction" auditable instead of an opinion,
# and they are also what retired the 5% assumption the task started from.
# B29 came out of the same scan and is recorded unfixed, which is section B
# working as intended.
#
# 41,000 had drifted to 0.4% slack under this change -- 40,853 against
# 41,000 -- the same "fires on the next legitimate sentence" state the
# seventh and eighth raises were each correcting. 46,000 against 40,853 is
# ~12.6%, inside the band.
#
# The EIGHTH covers V110 and V111 with their records (T96, T97, B25): the
# rate badge published two projections unmarked, then divided them by a
# wall-clock span that counted sleep. Both invariants carry the
# measurement that justifies them, and that is the part that had to stay
# -- the inter-turn gap percentiles across 11 transcripts are what make a
# 300s idle cap auditable instead of a magic constant, while V26 keeps
# the method in the commit. Decoration was trimmed first (the per-session
# turn list, the argument-count rationale); the numbers were not.
#
# Worth recording: the seventh had already drifted to 0.5% slack before
# this landed -- 35,813 against 36,000 -- which is the same "fires on the
# next legitimate sentence" state the seventh raise was itself
# correcting. 41,000 against 36,400 is ~12.6%, inside the band.
# History: 19,542 -> 20,500 (T49) -> 21,500 -> 25,000 -> 27,500 -> 31,000
# -> 34,000 -> 36,000 -> 41,000 -> 46,000 -> 52,000. Ten raises, every addition correct: the spec grows
# because the project does (103 invariants). Why each raise was justified
# lives in its commit, not here (V26).
#
# The sixth covered FIVE bug records in one day -- B17 (the published
# .crate failing its own suite), B18 (a merge commit read as empty), B19
# (`-C` is not isolation), B20 (a coverage badge no machine could
# reproduce) and B21 (hk silent after a failed step). Section B is
# APPEND-ONLY by nature, so a fixed ceiling over it is a promise to
# re-review at a fixed interval rather than a cap that can hold; that is
# the ceiling working, not failing.
#
# The SEVENTH corrects the sixth rather than absorbing new content, and it
# is recorded that way on purpose. 34,000 was set against 31,586 -- ~7.1%
# slack, UNDER the 10-13% this file asks for two paragraphs above. The
# shortfall was noticed and shipped anyway, which is the rule being read
# and then not applied. 36,000 against 32,033 is ~11%, inside the band.
#
# Worth keeping as the lesson: a ceiling set below its own slack rule is
# the "fires on the next legitimate sentence" failure the rule exists to
# prevent (V71), and B17 landing a day later would have fired it. The rule
# was right and the number was wrong; the fix is the number.
#
# The fifth was REVIEWED, not reflexive, and the review shed 855 chars
# before it moved the number: T90 and T91 described machinery that no
# longer exists (`cavekit-spec`, the ported guard), and V26 says method
# lives in the commit trail, so they trimmed to what+cites. The raise
# covers what remained -- V104 and B14, a new invariant class with its bug
# record, which is the spec growing because the project did.
#
# 31,000 against 27,724 is ~12% slack, per the rule above. The previous
# ceiling had drifted to 242, which is not a ceiling: it fires on the next
# legitimate sentence and trains the reflex it exists to catch (V71).
#
# README 5,700 -> 6,500, ONE reviewed raise, and the review found something
# worth writing down. What it buys is small and legitimate: `doctor
# --session` and its advice block, plus the `rate` badge's gauge, growth
# and `~to compaction` -- four verbs' worth of surface that landed in one
# stretch, each of which the generated command reference must describe.
#
# What the review actually found is that the reference is no longer a
# reference. The `rate` blurb alone runs ~2,600 characters and recites four
# invariants in full; a first visitor reads it before anything else in that
# section. That is V71's failure -- text that always appears is text nobody
# reads -- and the ceiling is the thing that noticed. The fix is compacting
# `src/docs.rs` back to what a command reference is for, and it is NOT this
# commit's job; it is recorded here so the next raise meets a known cause
# rather than treating the growth as inevitable.
#
# 6,500 against 5,753 is ~13%, at the top of the band this file asks for --
# deliberately, because the blurb compaction above should REDUCE this
# number, and a ceiling set tight against text about to shrink would fire
# on the next legitimate sentence instead.
#
# README 4k -> 5k, ONE reviewed raise covering a documented set, not six
# reflexive bumps as each section lands. What it buys, measured: the
# LLM-provenance block (494), then `Why`, `Guarantees`, `Status`, `Use it as
# a library` and `The name` -- the sections a first visitor actually reads,
# and the ones this README lacked while going public. `Status` in particular
# is where V107's publication-is-not-a-rung reasoning reaches someone who
# will never open the spec.
#
# This is the ceiling working as designed (V84): it fired, the addition was
# justified item by item, and the number moved once with the reason in the
# commit rather than here (V26). A README that grows because the project went
# public is the project growing; a README that grows every week is not, and
# the next raise should be asked to prove which it is.
SPEC.md    52000
README.md  5800
